Date: Fri, 15 Jan 93 16:49:33 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

I just posted a long (9000 word) paper to ExI-essay, titled:

      SHOULD PRIVATELY ENFORCED LAWS FIX PUNISHMENT OR RESTITUTION?
		(or, Why Not Let Convicts Hire the Police?)

and thought I'd try to give the rest of you an executive summary.

With a private civil law, a complaining victim expects to gain a
restitution from the guilty criminal, who expects to suffer a punishment.
This wealth transfer mainly comes from a fine which the criminal owes to
the victim.  The difference, punishment minus restitution, is the cost of
the law enforcement, and is due to two factors:

1) The cost of enforcing the law, including costs of investigation,
   pursuit, court proceedings, and defense/prosecution..
2) Risks imposed on criminals who may suffer a small chance of a 
   of a large fine if convicted, and similar risks for victims.

What do we want and expect from a negotiated private law?  We want the law
to try to minimize the cost of law enforcement, by competitive
subcontracting when possible.  And we expect a private law to try and set
restitution and punishment to optimize the incentives of criminal to avoid
the crime, and the victim to prevent it, if in fact the crime is a net bad,
or to encourage the "crime" if it is a net good (like the lost hiker who
breaks into a locked cabin).

The size of each party's stake in the crime, their net expected payoff if
the crime happens, should be in proportion to their relative influence over
whether the crime will occur.  If the criminal has most of the influence,
then the victim should just be compensated for about the amount of their
damage.

That all said, we come to the basic problem of what exactly the law should
fix.  If the law fixed a FINE amount for each crime, without knowing the
probability of conviction, it would have poor control over the restitution
and punishment each party expects on average, and over the amount spent on
law enforcement.

If the law fixed PUNISHMENT, then official estimates of the probability of
conviction, and of the risk-aversion of the criminal, would be required to
set the actual amount of fine imposed.  Restitution would then vary with
enforcement costs, which victims would work to minimize if they could
choose the enforcer.  (David Friedman proposed this option in 'Efficient
Institutions for the Private Enforcement of Law', J. Legal Studies, June,
379-397, 1984)

If the law instead fixed RESTITUTION, then official victim risk-aversion
estimates would be required in place of estimates for criminals.  Since the
criminal would here suffer the variation in enforcement costs, the criminal
could safely be placed in charge of choosing an enforcer.

More generally, the law might fix some way to TRADEOFF restitution vs.
punishment, to better deal with uncertain enforcement costs.  But this
would require risk-aversion estimates for both parties, a general auction
to choose the enforcer, and be considerably more complex.  What's a law to
do?

There are many things to note.  First, while the criminal cannot cheaply
insure against the risk of being caught due to moral hazard considerations,
no such problem limits victim insurance, and so we can neglect risks to
victims due to uncertainty of conviction.  Second, if criminals bribe
enforcers to avoid conviction, that raises the actual punishment (and
sometimes restitution) above the officially calculated ones.  

Third, it should be easier for victims to directly purchase consideration
to be treated with dignity, privacy, etc., from the enforcer, than for
criminals in hiding from conviction.  All of these considerations argue for
fixing restitution, so that the victim is in charge of enforcement.
(Actually, I do not know how to validate case-specific risk-aversion
estimates in any other case.)

But how can a criminal select an enforcer while hiding from conviction?
Unless they signal somehow "at the scene of the crime", the law would have
to appoint a criminal's agent, one with clear incentives to choose the
enforcer which would minimize punishment.
 
My longer paper describes how such an agent could be given such clear
incentives.  Basically, convicted criminals are sometimes randomly freed,
and their agents are then required to pay them enough to be willing to
again suffer a similar punishment.  The paper also describes how bets can
validate enforcer estimates of probability of conviction.  (David Friedman
proposed validating probabilities through frequencies in bundles of similar
crimes, and proposed no method for estimating risk-aversion.) 

My paper goes on to consider the case where law is contingent, so that the
legal punishment or restitution is not decided until a "crime type" is
determined at conviction.  This allows us to deal with the case where
someone falsely claims to be a crime victim.  But it requires official
estimates of the probabilities of different crime types, and so I describe
how such estimates could be validated, again with bets. 

