Date: Fri, 15 Jan 93 18:54:03 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Uplifting inventions

I enjoyed an uplifting experience today:  I spent hours pouring through old
patent files.  Does that sound dull?  Let me explain why it isn't.

While we are surrounded every day by the products of human ingenuity, we
see only the tip of the iceberg.  For every invention that makes it to
market, there are thousands of others that languish unused in the PTO's
files.  Now, many of these patents never reach mass production for good
reason--they simply fail to satisfy others' needs.  But I suspect that many
others are simply overlooked.  Too many inventors fail to realize that
getting a patent on a good idea doesn't guarantee that manufacturers will
chase you down and beg for production rights.  You have to *sell* your
inventions.

No matter what the individual merits of the thousands of patents I saw
today, however, the sheer mass of the PTO's files impressed me.  There are
a *whole lot* of patents out there--well over 5 million U.S. ones alone. 
This cold figure may fail to inspire you, but experiencing it in hardcopy
is another thing altogether.  Ignore for the moment the questionable status
of statist legal monopolies in patented inventions, and the PTO's role as
yet another federal bureacracy.  Imagine running your finger along the
spines of shelves and shelves of old PTO GAZETTEs, each one holding
thousands of patents.  If you've ever doubted the power of human ingenuity
and determination, this exposure to a concentrated dose of invention will
surely cure you.




