Subject: Kipling,Fermi,etc.

RHe told me that this poem, critical as it was of British education
(the "copybook" was the writing instruction system favored, the
copybook headings were little aphorisms at the top of every page, and
the "Gods of the Copybook" were the teachers, the chief conveyors of
the British worldview), did not make Kipling's publishers or peers
happy. "Too cynical."S (Timothy May, on RThe Gods of the Copybook
HeadingsS)

Why do you describe it as critical of British education? I would have
said that the point of the poem is the truth of the copybook headings
(phrases such as "If you don't work you will die.') The gods of the
copybook headings are the good guys, repeating the trite but true
constraints of life, in opposition to the gods of the market, who are
the unrealistic utopians. The poem (an old favorite of mine, by the
way) is profoundly conservative (one might almost say
anti-extropian)--as are the copybook headings.

"Perhaps aliens, and we too, actually evolve a concience." (dj on the
Fermi paradox)

I think this suggests one approach to the Fermi paradox. Perhaps
there are some species characteristics which are necessary to survive
during some stages of development and then limit later stages.
Suppose any species whose members are not all moral (defined, for the
moment, as very relucatant to impose large costs on others) ends up
destroying itself at the point in its development where individuals
have the ability (via engineering grey goo or some equivalent) to do
things that wipe out the species. The species is then stuck with
being moral when it gets past that stage--and as a result it does not
wipe out (or even show itself to?) less advanced species.

This is a sample of how such a mechanism might work--not a terribly
plausible sample, but I think a more plausible class of mechanisms.

"Since you haven't experienced other lifestyles, how can you be so
sure that extropianism is right?" (dj)

Well, I spend a bit over a week a year in the middle ages. Cooked a
nice 13th c. Andalusian desert over an open fire this August.

"> 8.  Perhaps a free market system results in catastrophe.  Oil
tanker
> spills. Nuclear accident.  Pollution.  AIDS or other fatal virus. 
There
> are so many disaster scenarios that a free market system makes
possible,
> almost certain.  (dj)
                                                                  
Does statism make disasters any less likely?  I think not, because
regulators don't pay for their mistakes." (Anton Sherwood)

So we have a possible explanation of the Fermi paradox. The free
market system is better than all alternatives but still
catastrophically bad, hence no complicated society can survive. In a
post some time back I suggested reasons why that might conceivably be
true. In essence, a working market system requires a reasonably good
definition of private property rights, and as we get richer and more
powerful, it may become harder and harder to draw appropriate
boundaries around property. If there is no good way of defining
property rights a market works poorly--even though it may be the best
alternative available.

