Date: Fri, 15 Jan 1993 23:11:54 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Re:  PPL: Why Not Let Convicts Run the Police?

> With a private civil law, a complaining victim expects to gain a
> restitution from the guilty criminal, who expects to suffer a punishment.
> This wealth transfer mainly comes from a fine which the criminal owes to
> the victim.  The difference, punishment minus restitution, is the cost of
> the law enforcement...

> Robin Hanson

Once again, we may be using different terminology.  I have always considered
"restitution" and "punishment" to be completely different concepts.
Restitution means, to me, the act of restoring the balance between the
parties to what it would have been if the crime had not taken place.
Punishment, in contrast, is the infliction of loss on the criminal in a
manner which does not restore anything to the victim.  If we accept these
definitions, it follows that the only legal systems a Libertarian society
would consider legitimate are those that produce restitution, NOT
punishment.  

Once restitution is complete, the legal job is finished.  (Yes, full
restitution does include investigation, legal fees and court costs.  The
criminal could avoid legal and court costs by settling out of court.  He
could also avoid investigation costs by admitting the crime before
investigation.)  If a court imposes punishment on top of restitution, it has
now become a criminal organization itself--the scales, by definition, are no
longer balanced.  The former criminal, now a victim, can now legitimately
sue for restitution.

As an aside, if the criminal agrees to settle out of court, but the victim
demands a higher amount of restitution than the criminal is willing to pay,
they go to court anyway.  If the court rules that the amount of restitution
the defendant had offered was sufficient compensation, the plaintiff would
be responsible for court costs and defendant's legal costs, since the trial
would not have taken place if the plaintiff had not tried to take more than
he was owed.

Incidentally, if the criminal IS punished (whether directly by the victim,
or by the victim's agent, the court) the victim has just undermined his own
case.  The harm done to the criminal must now be subtracted from the harm
done by the criminal in order to balance the scale.

At least, that's my opinion.

