Both Latvian words are almost certainly borrowings from Greek (through the
International Scientific Vocabulary).

I read once that CRYSTAL comes from KRU-ST- meaning `coldest'; the Greeks
figured that to stay frozen permanently, that ice must have been awfully
cold!

- - -
Tim says:
> The study of the precursors to our modern languages (and concepts)
> seems more exciting to me than the study of mere physical artifacts.
> What's a flint axe or a Venus statuette compared to tracing the origin
> of a concept back into Old Norse, Germanic, Hittite, and Sanskrit?

> Anton Sherwood should have something to say in this thread, I hope.

I was tempted to just say I agree with the foregoing paragraph.

I am particularly interested in naming.  A little book called "Words and
Places" (1896), by a clergyman named Isaac Taylor, contains the most
remarkable tidbits.  Did you know that there are a lot of Arabic names in
the Alps, and English names in northern France?  That London used to put up
a maypole at the church of St Andrew Undershaft?  Or -- an easy one -- why a
novelist must never put a "-thorpe" in Dorset?

- - - 
The language family under discussion is usually called Indo-European
[henceforth IE], because it stretches from India to Europe (with
interruptions).  It has also been (may still be, in German) called
Indo-Germanic, after its two most distant branches, Indic and Germanic.

The book "An Introduction to the Indo-European Languages" by Philip Baldi
lists these branches:
	Italic (Latin, its poorly attested sisters, and descendants)
	Celtic (Welsh, Gaelic, ...)
	Indo-Iranian (Sanskrit, Persian, ...)
	Hellenic (Greek)
	Armenian
	Albanian
	Baltic (Latvian, Lithuanian)
	Slavic (Russian, Czech, ...)
	Germanic (Gothic, German, Scandinavian, English)
	Tocharian
	Anatolian (Hittite, ...)

Coarser lumpings are controversial.  There is evidence both for and against
linking Baltic with Slavic, Italic with Celtic.  There is evidence both for
and against an overall binary division between western "centum" languages
and eastern "satem" languages (named after the word for 100 in Latin and
Avestan).

For ages I have wished for a handy grammar of Proto-IE (we probably can't
say what word order was, but much is known about declension and
conjugation); but such a book, if it exists, may only be in German, which I
don't read.  Sigh.  The books and articles referenced in Baldi's book seem
less comprehensive than what I want.  Yet students of IE must learn all this
stuff somehow!

